WEBVTT

00:03.840 --> 00:04.160
All right.

00:04.160 --> 00:06.640
So now we have very similar function to the data part.

00:06.680 --> 00:08.200
We have the date name.

00:08.200 --> 00:12.520
So the only difference here is that it returns the name of the date part.

00:12.560 --> 00:12.880
All right.

00:12.880 --> 00:14.000
So now back to our example.

00:14.000 --> 00:18.560
We have learned we can extract different types of parts from one date.

00:18.560 --> 00:21.440
But we learned as well that all of them are numbers.

00:21.440 --> 00:24.680
How about we would like to extract the name of the month.

00:24.680 --> 00:30.000
So instead of eight I would like to get the name of the month like August or instead of the 20.

00:30.040 --> 00:34.720
I would like to get the day name like here in this example it's going to be Wednesday.

00:34.720 --> 00:39.640
So in order to get the name of the parts, we have to use the function date name.

00:39.640 --> 00:44.880
So for example, if you use the function date name using the part month, you will not get eight in

00:44.920 --> 00:45.440
the output.

00:45.440 --> 00:48.240
You will get the full name of the month August.

00:48.240 --> 00:52.240
So as you can see we are getting a string, a full name and as well the same thing.

00:52.240 --> 00:56.400
If you use date name for the weekday, you will not get 20.

00:56.440 --> 00:57.720
Like the day function.

00:57.720 --> 01:02.080
You will get the name of the day Wednesday and as well here the output is string.

01:02.080 --> 01:03.440
So as you can see it's very simple.

01:03.440 --> 01:09.600
We are using the date name in order to get the name of the parts and the data type of the output here

01:09.600 --> 01:10.400
is string.

01:10.400 --> 01:11.640
It is not an integer.

01:11.640 --> 01:16.720
So as you can see here, we have different types of functions that all of them are doing the same job.

01:16.720 --> 01:20.120
We are extracting parts from one date.

01:20.160 --> 01:20.400
Okay.

01:20.440 --> 01:25.560
So now by checking the data name syntax is going to be identical to the date part.

01:25.600 --> 01:31.720
So we are just switching the function name it needs from you to define the part and as will the dates.

01:31.720 --> 01:35.440
The only difference is that you are getting different data type at the output.

01:35.440 --> 01:37.920
So here we are getting a string instead of integer.

01:37.960 --> 01:38.240
All right.

01:38.240 --> 01:39.440
So now let's check the date name.

01:39.440 --> 01:41.280
It is very similar to the date part.

01:41.320 --> 01:43.040
So we're going to have it like this.

01:43.040 --> 01:45.000
We're going to work as well with the creation time.

01:45.360 --> 01:47.120
So we're going to say date name.

01:47.400 --> 01:49.840
And then after that we have to define the parts.

01:49.840 --> 01:51.720
So let's go for example with the month.

01:52.040 --> 01:55.080
And our field is as usual the creation time.

01:55.120 --> 01:58.680
And let's call it month date name.

01:59.800 --> 02:00.480
Like this.

02:00.680 --> 02:01.960
So that's it.

02:01.960 --> 02:03.400
Let's go and execute it.

02:03.400 --> 02:06.240
Now if you go to the output over here, you can see we have the month.

02:06.240 --> 02:08.360
But this time we don't have numbers.

02:08.360 --> 02:10.200
We have the full name of the month.

02:10.200 --> 02:14.200
So we have January February, March instead of having one two, three.

02:14.200 --> 02:17.350
So this is the big difference between the date name and date part.

02:17.390 --> 02:17.870
Date part.

02:17.910 --> 02:19.230
You get numbers.

02:19.230 --> 02:19.510
Date.

02:19.550 --> 02:19.790
Name.

02:19.790 --> 02:21.510
You get the name of the part.

02:21.550 --> 02:23.430
So let's do the same thing for the day.

02:23.430 --> 02:26.310
We would like to get the name of the day.

02:26.470 --> 02:28.070
So I'm just duplicating it.

02:28.070 --> 02:31.870
But now in order to get the full name of the day, we cannot go with the day.

02:31.910 --> 02:35.030
We're going to go with the week day as a part.

02:35.070 --> 02:36.110
So that's it.

02:36.110 --> 02:38.150
I will call it week day.

02:38.590 --> 02:39.790
So let's execute it now.

02:39.790 --> 02:43.590
As you can see in the outputs we have here a new column called weekday.

02:43.590 --> 02:47.230
And inside it we have the name of the day instead of a number.

02:47.230 --> 02:49.790
So here we have Wednesday, Sunday Friday and so on.

02:49.790 --> 02:51.830
So the full name of the day.

02:52.110 --> 02:55.150
Of course with the day let's go and try that out.

02:57.150 --> 02:59.350
So this is the day of the month.

02:59.350 --> 03:02.070
And of course the day of the month has no name.

03:02.070 --> 03:04.470
And SQL of course can return the numbers again.

03:04.470 --> 03:06.910
So you can see one, five, ten, 20 and so on.

03:06.910 --> 03:12.830
But still there is a difference between the day from the day name and the day from the date parts.

03:12.830 --> 03:15.510
In the date parts we are getting integers.

03:15.510 --> 03:19.990
So if you store those information in a new table, it's going to be stored as an integer.

03:20.190 --> 03:25.710
But in the day that you are getting from the date name, it is a number, but still it can be stored

03:25.710 --> 03:27.150
as a string value.

03:27.150 --> 03:30.110
So the data type of those numbers is a string.

03:30.110 --> 03:33.830
And the data types of the day from the date part is an integer.

03:33.830 --> 03:36.670
And the same thing can happen if you extract, for example, a year.

03:36.670 --> 03:39.430
So you don't have like a full text of the year.

03:39.710 --> 03:42.390
So let me just do it like this.

03:42.390 --> 03:46.670
So if we say a year, you will not get the name of the year.

03:46.670 --> 03:49.190
You're still getting the numbers, the digits.

03:49.190 --> 03:51.230
But the data type here is a string.

03:51.230 --> 03:52.030
So that's it.

03:52.030 --> 03:54.630
This is the difference between the date name and the date parts.

03:54.910 --> 03:56.590
For the month and weekday.

03:56.590 --> 03:58.710
You will get the full name for the other stuff.

03:58.710 --> 04:01.870
You will get numbers but with the string data type.

04:01.870 --> 04:07.910
So the most important thing about the date name is to present easy to read and human readable informations

04:07.910 --> 04:08.830
to the users.

04:08.830 --> 04:13.550
So imagine you are building a report called Sales by month and then you show to the user the month is

04:13.590 --> 04:15.830
as numbers one, two, three until 12.

04:15.870 --> 04:21.550
This is of course okay, but it is way more nicer if you present those informations as a full text.

04:21.550 --> 04:26.830
So you go with the date name in order to show instead of one you show January, February, March and

04:26.830 --> 04:27.990
the full name of the month.

04:27.990 --> 04:31.390
And this is going to look way nicer in reporting for the users.

04:31.390 --> 04:34.190
So this is the core use case of the date name.
